<%# The attention band: what needs you, then where the money actually went.
    Local: `presenter` (HomePresenter). See the UI design spec §4.2.

    Three kinds of problem, in descending loudness: an overdrawn account (no other band can
    report it — see HomePresenter#overdrawn_accounts), a pool with no account that is
    actually asking for money this period (nothing can fund it, and it is deliberately
    absent from the waterfall below), then the envelopes whose own status needs attention,
    in the priority order the user ranked them in. `uniq` because a pool can be both
    unassigned and overdrawn.

    `orphan_pools_owed`, NOT `orphan_pools` — the middle term is the one that changed and
    this is why. Every orphan used to count here regardless of status, so a healthy user
    with five account-less savings goals opened the root route on "You're covered this
    period" above "5 things need you", with five rows saying nothing can fund goals that
    were asking for nothing. An account-less savings pool was the ORDINARY shape then, so
    that was not an edge; it was the common screen.

    IT IS NO SCREEN AT ALL NOW, and the sentence above used to say "is the ORDINARY shape
    until Plan 3's backfill". The backfill has run: every pool names an account, the model
    refuses a new orphan and so does a CHECK constraint, so both `orphan_pools_owed` and
    `orphan_pools` are empty for every user and neither term below contributes a row. The
    narrowing is still the right narrowing and is recorded because the follow-up that
    deletes the apparatus has to delete a middle term that is CORRECT rather than one that
    was a bug. Pool::REFUSALS carries that follow-up's list, and this file is on it.

    `orphan_pools_owed` is exactly the set whose ask #orphan_required is made of, so this
    heading now counts the same pools the standing band's clause above already names — one
    set, said twice, rather than two sets a reader has to reconcile. And it loses nothing:
    an orphan that is ALSO in trouble on its own terms (overdrawn, overdue, behind) is
    already in `attention_pools` and still counted, still labelled "no account · …" by
    `pool_problem_label`. What drops out is only the quiet orphan asking for nothing — and
    the orphans band below still lists every one of them under a heading that says these
    have no account, which is where "these have no account" belongs. %>
<% orphans = presenter.orphan_pools %>
<% problems = (presenter.overdrawn_accounts + presenter.orphan_pools_owed + presenter.attention_pools).uniq %>
<% rows = presenter.waterfall %>

<%# The cutoff marks where the cash ran out, which is AFTER the last pool that received any
    of it: a pool funded $355 of $400 did get money, and the spec's §4.2 waterfall draws the
    line beneath it.

    ASKED, NOT DECIDED HERE. That rule and its `|| rows.length` fallback used to be written out
    in this line and again in distributions/_waterfall.html.erb — one money-shaped decision with
    two readers, both in ERB. HomePresenter#cutoff carries the gate this band needs (one account,
    and something actually short); Waterfall.cutoff carries the rule, for both screens. %>
<% cutoff = presenter.cutoff %>

<section class="bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded" aria-labelledby="attention-heading">
  <div class="px-6 py-4 border-b border-gray-200">
    <h3 id="attention-heading" class="text-sm font-semibold text-gray-900">
      <% if problems.any? %>
        <%= pluralize(problems.size, "thing") %> need<%= "s" if problems.one? %> you
      <% elsif presenter.covered? %>
        Nothing needs you
      <% else %>
        <%# Real state, not a corner: every rate envelope reads `left_to_spend`, so a period
            can be short with no pool flagged. "Nothing needs you" above a gap is the exact
            failure this band exists to prevent. %>
        Nothing is flagged, but you're still short
      <% end %>
    </h3>
  </div>

  <% problems.each do |pool| %>
    <% status = presenter.status_for(pool) %>
    <% fix = presenter.fix_for(pool) %>
    <div class="px-6 py-3 border-b border-gray-100 <%= "bg-status-danger-light" if status.state == :overdrawn %>"
         data-problem-pool="<%= pool.name %>">
      <div class="flex justify-between items-baseline">
        <span class="font-medium text-gray-900"><%= pool.name %></span>
        <span class="text-sm text-status-danger">
          <%# `changed_after_distributing:` through the presenter, never a query of the partial's
              own — same reason as `status` above: the period this compares against is
              `presenter.today`'s, and a band reaching for `Date.current` here would date the
              distribution differently from every other figure on the screen. Silent on every
              state but `:behind` (HomeHelper#pool_status_label owns that gate). %>
          <%= pool_problem_label(status,
                                 orphan: orphans.include?(pool),
                                 changed_after_distributing: presenter.changed_after_distributing?(pool)) %>
        </span>
      </div>

      <%# THE FIX (spec §4.2). Every problem gains a specific, clickable answer naming a source
          that can genuinely cover it — or, when nothing can, says so in words rather than
          offering a dead button.

          FIVE BRANCHES, AND EACH IS A DIFFERENT ANSWER RATHER THAN THE SAME ONE SAID FIVE WAYS.
          Four of them decline to offer a button, and they decline for reasons that send the
          reader to four different places, so collapsing them would be the same defect as a
          silent row:

          1. NO ACCOUNT. No account's money can reach this pool whatever anyone moves, and it has
             no gap to name either — PoolStatus#amount is that pool's own BALANCE in the quiet
             states an owed orphan reaches, which read "Retirement Supplement … $545.00" on the
             demo seeds. HomePresenter#fix_for returns nil for it.
          2. THE MONEY IS ALREADY THERE. An overdue bill fires on a DATE and a missing payment,
             not on the balance, so an envelope holding every penny of its premium is still red.
             PoolStatus#funding_gap is zero, and the honest answer is that the bill needs paying
             rather than funding — the same sentence the distribution screen's alerts band prints
             about the same pool, in the same voice. Offering to move money in here would have
             the user make a real mistake to fix an imaginary problem.
          3. THE MONEY IS COMING. Same principle one step out: the waterfall below this row funds
             this pool's whole ask, so a move today would rob one envelope to solve something the
             next distribution solves by itself. Read off that same waterfall rather than
             recomputed, so the two bands cannot contradict each other — see
             HomePresenter#covered_by_waterfall?.
          4. NOTHING CAN COVER IT. Every sibling was asked; see HomeHelper#fix_gap_sentence.
          5. A SOURCE — the button, and its consequence.

          The consequence line is PoolMovementsHelper's, off PoolReallocationPresenter::Candidate —
          the same sentence, from the same Data object, that the screen this button opens will
          print half a second later. There is one answer to "what would this move cost", and
          PoolReallocationPresenter::source_order is likewise the one answer to "from where". %>
      <% if fix.nil? %>
        <p class="text-sm text-gray-600 mt-2">Assign it to an account before any money can reach it.</p>
      <% elsif !fix.needs_money? %>
        <p class="text-sm text-gray-600 mt-2">Its money is already there — this needs paying, not funding.</p>
      <% elsif fix.covered? %>
        <p class="text-sm text-gray-600 mt-2">Its money is coming — the next distribution funds this in full.</p>
      <% else %>
        <% fix_path = { to_pool_id: pool.id, amount: fix.amount_param } %>
        <div class="mt-2">
          <% if fix.source %>
            <p class="text-sm text-gray-600 mb-2">This has to come from money you already have.</p>
            <div class="flex flex-wrap gap-2">
              <%= link_to fix_button_label(fix),
                          new_pool_movement_path(**fix_path, from_pool_id: fix.source.id),
                          class: "btn btn-primary" %>
              <%# No `from_pool_id`: the reallocation screen takes any subset of the three, so this
                  arrives with the destination and the amount filled in and every source still to
                  choose from — including the ones this band declined to propose. %>
              <%= link_to "Take from another pool…",
                          new_pool_movement_path(**fix_path),
                          class: "btn btn-secondary" %>
            </div>
            <p class="text-sm mt-2 <%= reallocation_damage_class(fix.candidate) %>">
              <%= reallocation_pool_name(fix.source) %> <%= reallocation_damage_sentence(fix.candidate) %>
            </p>
          <% else %>
            <p class="text-sm text-gray-600"><%= fix_gap_sentence(fix) %></p>
          <% end %>
        </div>
      <% end %>
    </div>
  <% end %>

  <%# Whenever there is a gap OR something needs you.

      It used to be the gap alone, and that suppressed the plan on exactly the screen that
      needed it: `won't make it · Aug 18` rendered under "You're covered this period" with no
      way to see that this period's distribution funds the bill in full. PoolStatus describes
      the pool NOW, the waterfall describes the plan, and showing the warning while hiding its
      own resolution is the worst combination of the two.

      Still silent when nothing is short and nothing is flagged, which is principle 3 intact:
      there is no consequence to report on a screen with no problems on it.

      And `rows.any?` first, because "something needs you" is NOT the same question as "is
      there a plan to show". Two of the three kinds of problem — an overdrawn account and an
      account-less pool — have no waterfall row at all, and the zero-need reject can empty the
      list outright, so this heading could render over nothing. On a money screen an empty
      headed band reads as data that failed to load. %>
  <% if rows.any? && (!presenter.covered? || problems.any?) %>
    <div class="px-6 py-4 border-t border-gray-200" aria-labelledby="waterfall-heading">
      <%# Sentence case, not the `uppercase` treatment this codebase gives short labels like
          "Current Balance": that renders as WHERE YOUR MONEY WENT, which shouts a whole
          sentence — and it is the literal copy the design spec asks for. %>
      <%# Present tense, not "went": this band has never described a completed distribution —
          it describes what the NEXT one would do. Past tense was tolerable while it only
          appeared on a short screen, where a shortfall reads like something that already
          happened to you; it now renders on covered screens too, above a plan that plainly
          has not run. The design spec is being corrected to match. %>
      <%# THE TWO BANDS ANSWER DIFFERENT QUESTIONS AND MUST SAY SO. The rows above offer
          `Take $553.85` — PoolStatus#funding_gap, the whole cumulative hole, read from the live
          balance. The rows below print `$35.00 of $171.43` — #required, THIS period's share of
          it, post-sweep. Both figures are right and both are wanted (see
          HomePresenter#fix_amount_for), and on the demo they sit inches apart on one screen with
          nothing telling the reader they are measured over different spans. That is the bridge,
          and it belongs in the band's own framing rather than as a seventh clause on every row:
          "the whole hole" above, "what this period puts in it" below. %>
      <div class="flex items-baseline justify-between gap-4 mb-2">
        <div>
          <p id="waterfall-heading" class="text-xs font-semibold tracking-wide text-gray-500">Where your money goes</p>
          <p class="text-xs text-gray-500">
            This period's share — what the next distribution puts in, not the whole gap.
          </p>
        </div>
        <%# THE ACTION THIS BAND IS DESCRIBING, one click away. Home tells a user "the next
            distribution funds this in full" and, until this link existed, gave them no way to
            reach it: `new_distribution_path` appeared nowhere in `app/` outside the distributions
            controller and its own views, so the only way to split a paycheck was to type the URL.
            The sidebar carries the same link for the screens where this band does not render.

            `turbo_prefetch: false` because this GET takes write locks — see
            shared/_nav_section.html.erb, where the same link says why at length. %>
        <%= link_to "Distribute this period",
                    new_distribution_path,
                    class: "btn btn-secondary",
                    data: { turbo_prefetch: false } %>
      </div>

      <%# Two groups rather than a flag inside one loop: the cutoff is then written once and
          renders correctly at either edge — before every row when the first pool got nothing,
          and after the last row when the money ran out inside it. A nil cutoff (several
          accounts) leaves one group, and no line. %>
      <% groups = cutoff ? [rows.first(cutoff), rows.drop(cutoff)] : [rows] %>
      <% groups.each_with_index do |group, index| %>
        <% if index == 1 %>
          <div class="border-t-2 border-dashed border-status-danger my-2 pt-2 text-sm text-status-danger font-medium">
            — ran out here · <%= number_to_currency(presenter.shortfall) %> unfunded —
          </div>
        <% end %>
        <% group.each do |row| %>
          <div class="flex justify-between text-sm py-1">
            <span class="text-gray-900"><%= row[:pool].name %></span>
            <span class="text-gray-600">
              <%= number_to_currency(row[:funded]) %> of <%= number_to_currency(row[:needed]) %>
            </span>
          </div>
        <% end %>
      <% end %>
    </div>
  <% end %>
</section>
